{"\ufeffNYKRIS \u2014 Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam Question Bank\n200 randomized-ready educational questions for gameplay skips\n\nFormat: 75 True/False + 75 MCQs + 50 One-Word/Very Short Answer. The questions cover the meaning, Indian cultural context, unity, peace, global citizenship, environment, diversity, cooperation, and contemporary relevance of Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam.\nSection A \u2014 True / False (75)\n1. \u201cVasudhaiva Kutumbakam\u201d is commonly translated as \u201cThe world is one family.\u201d\nAnswer: True\n2. The phrase \u201cVasudhaiva Kutumbakam\u201d is associated with Sanskrit literature.\nAnswer: True\n3. The word \u201cVasudha\u201d refers to the Earth or world.\nAnswer: True\n4. \u201cKutumbakam\u201d refers to a family.\nAnswer: True\n5. The idea promotes respect for people beyond one's own community.\nAnswer: True\n6. Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam promotes hatred between nations.\nAnswer: False\n7. The concept encourages peaceful coexistence.\nAnswer: True\n8. The phrase teaches that humanity can be viewed as one family.\nAnswer: True\n9. Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam is a principle of universal brotherhood.\nAnswer: True\n10. The concept encourages discrimination based on nationality.\nAnswer: False\n11. The idea can support international cooperation.\nAnswer: True\n12. Respect for diversity is compatible with Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam.\nAnswer: True\n13. The concept is limited only to family members living in one house.\nAnswer: False\n14. Sanskrit is the language of the phrase Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam.\nAnswer: True\n15. The phrase is found in the Maha Upanishad.\nAnswer: True\n16. The idea is connected with Indian philosophical traditions.\nAnswer: True\n17. The concept rejects compassion toward other people.\nAnswer: False\n18. Helping people during a global crisis can reflect the spirit of Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam.\nAnswer: True\n19. The idea can be connected with peaceful relations among countries.\nAnswer: True\n20. Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam means \u201cOnly my country matters.\u201d\nAnswer: False\n21. The concept can encourage empathy.\nAnswer: True\n22. The principle can be applied to modern global citizenship.\nAnswer: True\n23. Environmental problems can affect people across national borders.\nAnswer: True\n24. Protecting shared natural resources can reflect global responsibility.\nAnswer: True\n25. The concept requires every culture to become identical.\nAnswer: False\n26. Unity means that cultural diversity must disappear.\nAnswer: False\n27. The idea can coexist with respect for different cultures.\nAnswer: True\n28. Peaceful dialogue can help resolve conflicts.\nAnswer: True\n29. Violence is the central teaching of Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam.\nAnswer: False\n30. The phrase contains Sanskrit words.\nAnswer: True\n31. The principle encourages people to think beyond narrow self-interest.\nAnswer: True\n32. Global cooperation is relevant to issues such as climate change.\nAnswer: True\n33. Human dignity can be respected regardless of nationality.\nAnswer: True\n34. The concept is relevant only to ancient India and has no modern application.\nAnswer: False\n35. The idea can encourage kindness toward strangers.\nAnswer: True\n36. International aid can be an example of global solidarity.\nAnswer: True\n37. The principle promotes cooperation during disasters.\nAnswer: True\n38. The concept encourages understanding between communities.\nAnswer: True\n39. Discrimination supports the spirit of universal family.\nAnswer: False\n40. The phrase can be used to express universal human connectedness.\nAnswer: True\n41. A global family can include people of different religions.\nAnswer: True\n42. A global family can include people of different languages.\nAnswer: True\n43. A global family can include people of different cultures.\nAnswer: True\n44. The concept requires everyone to share the same religion.\nAnswer: False\n45. Respecting another person's culture is consistent with the principle.\nAnswer: True\n46. The principle can encourage responsible use of Earth's resources.\nAnswer: True\n47. Clean water and air can be viewed as shared human concerns.\nAnswer: True\n48. Global citizenship involves awareness of responsibilities beyond one's immediate community.\nAnswer: True\n49. The concept discourages cooperation between countries.\nAnswer: False\n50. The idea can promote friendship among nations.\nAnswer: True\n51. The principle can encourage peaceful exchange of knowledge.\nAnswer: True\n52. Education can help people understand different cultures.\nAnswer: True\n53. Learning about other cultures necessarily destroys one's own culture.\nAnswer: False\n54. The concept can inspire service to others.\nAnswer: True\n55. The phrase emphasizes a broad sense of belonging.\nAnswer: True\n56. The principle can be relevant to humanitarian action.\nAnswer: True\n57. Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam means that every person must agree on every issue.\nAnswer: False\n58. Unity and uniformity mean exactly the same thing.\nAnswer: False\n59. People can be united while remaining culturally diverse.\nAnswer: True\n60. The concept can encourage mutual respect.\nAnswer: True\n61. The Earth is a shared home for humanity.\nAnswer: True\n62. The principle can support efforts to reduce prejudice.\nAnswer: True\n63. Prejudice is consistent with universal brotherhood.\nAnswer: False\n64. Cooperation can help communities respond to common challenges.\nAnswer: True\n65. Global health challenges can require international cooperation.\nAnswer: True\n66. The concept can encourage peaceful problem-solving.\nAnswer: True\n67. The principle is relevant to responsible digital citizenship as well.\nAnswer: True\n68. Online communities can also benefit from respect and empathy.\nAnswer: True\n69. The concept teaches that only powerful countries deserve respect.\nAnswer: False\n70. The idea can encourage dialogue between different communities.\nAnswer: True\n71. Respecting human rights is compatible with universal human dignity.\nAnswer: True\n72. The principle can encourage people to care about future generations.\nAnswer: True\n73. Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam promotes isolation from the rest of the world.\nAnswer: False\n74. The concept can encourage shared responsibility for the planet.\nAnswer: True\n75. The idea remains useful for discussing peace and global cooperation today.\nAnswer: True\n\nSection B \u2014 Multiple Choice Questions (75)\n1. What is the commonly accepted meaning of \u201cVasudhaiva Kutumbakam\u201d?\nA. The world is one family\nB. My nation is the only family\nC. Family is everything\nD. The Earth belongs to one ruler\nAnswer: A\n2. Which language is the phrase \u201cVasudhaiva Kutumbakam\u201d from?\nA. Hindi\nB. Sanskrit\nC. Tamil\nD. Persian\nAnswer: B\n3. What does \u201cVasudha\u201d refer to?\nA. Family\nB. Earth/world\nC. Friendship\nD. Peace\nAnswer: B\n4. What does \u201cKutumbakam\u201d mean?\nA. Nation\nB. Family\nC. Earth\nD. Knowledge\nAnswer: B\n5. The phrase is associated with which type of Indian literature?\nA. Sanskrit philosophical literature\nB. Modern newspapers\nC. Travel guides\nD. Scientific manuals\nAnswer: A\n6. Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam is found in which text?\nA. Maha Upanishad\nB. Arthashastra\nC. Rajatarangini\nD. Akbarnama\nAnswer: A\n7. Which value is most closely associated with the phrase?\nA. Universal brotherhood\nB. Isolation\nC. Competition\nD. Discrimination\nAnswer: A\n8. Which modern idea best reflects Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam?\nA. Global cooperation\nB. Complete isolation\nC. National rivalry\nD. Cultural hatred\nAnswer: A\n9. Which action best demonstrates the principle?\nA. Helping people during a disaster\nB. Ignoring other communities\nC. Spreading prejudice\nD. Refusing dialogue\nAnswer: A\n10. What does the phrase encourage people to see humanity as?\nA. One family\nB. Separate enemies\nC. One political party\nD. One language\nAnswer: A\n11. Which of these is NOT consistent with the principle?\nA. Empathy\nB. Respect\nC. Cooperation\nD. Discrimination\nAnswer: D\n12. Which global issue particularly requires cooperation?\nA. Climate change\nB. Personal handwriting\nC. School uniform\nD. Individual hobbies\nAnswer: A\n13. Respect for different cultures is an example of:\nA. Cultural understanding\nB. Isolation\nC. Prejudice\nD. Exclusion\nAnswer: A\n14. Which quality helps build a global family?\nA. Empathy\nB. Hatred\nC. Arrogance\nD. Prejudice\nAnswer: A\n15. What does universal brotherhood emphasize?\nA. Human connectedness\nB. National superiority\nC. Social exclusion\nD. Competition\nAnswer: A\n16. Which institution can promote international cooperation?\nA. United Nations\nB. A local shop\nC. A private diary\nD. A sports bag\nAnswer: A\n17. Which action promotes peaceful coexistence?\nA. Dialogue\nB. Violence\nC. Discrimination\nD. Threats\nAnswer: A\n18. What can education promote between cultures?\nA. Understanding\nB. Hatred\nC. Isolation\nD. Fear\nAnswer: A\n19. Which statement best describes cultural diversity?\nA. Different cultures can coexist respectfully\nB. All cultures must become identical\nC. Only one culture should exist\nD. Culture has no value\nAnswer: A\n20. Which is an example of global solidarity?\nA. Providing humanitarian aid\nB. Ignoring disasters\nC. Closing all communication\nD. Spreading rumours\nAnswer: A\n21. What is global citizenship mainly concerned with?\nA. Responsibilities toward the wider world\nB. Only personal wealth\nC. Avoiding all cultures\nD. Rejecting cooperation\nAnswer: A\n22. Which value is important when working with people from different backgrounds?\nA. Mutual respect\nB. Prejudice\nC. Suspicion\nD. Discrimination\nAnswer: A\n23. Which environmental action reflects shared responsibility?\nA. Reducing pollution\nB. Wasting water\nC. Destroying forests\nD. Littering\nAnswer: A\n24. Why is climate change a global concern?\nA. Its effects cross borders\nB. It affects only one person\nC. It has no environmental impact\nD. It occurs only indoors\nAnswer: A\n25. Which phrase best summarizes the spirit of Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam?\nA. Humanity is interconnected\nB. Everyone must be identical\nC. Only my group matters\nD. Countries should never cooperate\nAnswer: A\n26. What can peaceful dialogue prevent?\nA. Unnecessary conflict\nB. Friendship\nC. Understanding\nD. Cooperation\nAnswer: A\n27. Which of these can unite diverse communities?\nA. Shared goals\nB. Prejudice\nC. Hatred\nD. Exclusion\nAnswer: A\n28. What is empathy?\nA. Understanding another person's feelings\nB. Ignoring others\nC. Judging everyone\nD. Avoiding communication\nAnswer: A\n29. Which action shows respect for diversity?\nA. Listening to different perspectives\nB. Mocking another culture\nC. Rejecting languages\nD. Forcing uniformity\nAnswer: A\n30. Which is an example of international cooperation?\nA. Countries working together on disaster relief\nB. Countries refusing all contact\nC. Countries spreading misinformation\nD. Countries banning dialogue\nAnswer: A\n31. Which value is opposite to universal brotherhood?\nA. Discrimination\nB. Compassion\nC. Respect\nD. Cooperation\nAnswer: A\n32. How can students practise Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam?\nA. Respect classmates and cooperate\nB. Exclude new students\nC. Mock differences\nD. Refuse teamwork\nAnswer: A\n33. Which classroom activity best reflects the idea?\nA. Inclusive group work\nB. Bullying\nC. Exclusion\nD. Insults\nAnswer: A\n34. What does inclusion mean?\nA. Making people feel accepted and involved\nB. Keeping people apart\nC. Ignoring differences\nD. Rejecting newcomers\nAnswer: A\n35. Why is diversity valuable?\nA. It brings different perspectives\nB. It creates no learning\nC. It must be removed\nD. It prevents cooperation\nAnswer: A\n36. Which is a peaceful way to solve disagreements?\nA. Discussion\nB. Violence\nC. Insults\nD. Threats\nAnswer: A\n37. What can prejudice lead to?\nA. Unfair treatment\nB. Understanding\nC. Equality\nD. Cooperation\nAnswer: A\n38. Which principle encourages caring for others?\nA. Compassion\nB. Selfishness\nC. Exclusion\nD. Hostility\nAnswer: A\n39. Which is a shared global resource?\nA. Atmosphere\nB. One person's notebook\nC. A private toy\nD. A school desk\nAnswer: A\n40. What does responsible global citizenship include?\nA. Caring about global consequences\nB. Ignoring others\nC. Rejecting cooperation\nD. Spreading hatred\nAnswer: A\n41. Which action helps protect water?\nA. Using water responsibly\nB. Leaving taps running\nC. Polluting rivers\nD. Wasting clean water\nAnswer: A\n42. Which action supports peace?\nA. Respectful communication\nB. Provocation\nC. Violence\nD. Hate speech\nAnswer: A\n43. What can cultural exchange encourage?\nA. Mutual understanding\nB. Hostility\nC. Isolation\nD. Prejudice\nAnswer: A\n44. Which of these is a humanitarian value?\nA. Compassion\nB. Cruelty\nC. Exclusion\nD. Indifference\nAnswer: A\n45. Why can international cooperation help during pandemics?\nA. Countries can share knowledge and resources\nB. It prevents all communication\nC. It makes science unnecessary\nD. It stops all travel forever\nAnswer: A\n46. Which concept is closest to \u201cSarve Bhavantu Sukhinah\u201d?\nA. May all be happy\nB. Only I should be happy\nC. Only one nation should prosper\nD. Everyone should compete\nAnswer: A\n47. Which principle supports peaceful coexistence?\nA. Mutual respect\nB. Hatred\nC. Domination\nD. Discrimination\nAnswer: A\n48. What does \u201cunity in diversity\u201d suggest?\nA. Different people can live together respectfully\nB. Everyone must look the same\nC. Differences are harmful\nD. Only one culture matters\nAnswer: A\n49. Which action reduces social division?\nA. Dialogue and cooperation\nB. Rumours\nC. Stereotyping\nD. Exclusion\nAnswer: A\n50. What is tolerance?\nA. Respecting differences without hostility\nB. Forcing agreement\nC. Rejecting differences\nD. Ignoring all people\nAnswer: A\n51. Which action promotes friendship among nations?\nA. Cultural and educational exchange\nB. Threats\nC. Isolation\nD. Propaganda\nAnswer: A\n52. What is a major benefit of cooperation?\nA. Solving common problems\nB. Creating unnecessary conflict\nC. Increasing prejudice\nD. Preventing communication\nAnswer: A\n53. Which value can help resolve cultural misunderstandings?\nA. Open-mindedness\nB. Arrogance\nC. Stereotyping\nD. Hostility\nAnswer: A\n54. What does equality mean in a social context?\nA. People deserve equal dignity and rights\nB. Only powerful people matter\nC. Some cultures are superior\nD. Only wealthy people matter\nAnswer: A\n55. Which action shows global responsibility?\nA. Reducing waste\nB. Dumping waste into rivers\nC. Wasting electricity\nD. Destroying habitats\nAnswer: A\n56. Which type of leadership reflects universal family values?\nA. Inclusive leadership\nB. Discriminatory leadership\nC. Authoritarian exclusion\nD. Divisive leadership\nAnswer: A\n57. Why is dialogue important?\nA. It allows people to understand different viewpoints\nB. It guarantees everyone agrees\nC. It prevents communication\nD. It encourages prejudice\nAnswer: A\n58. Which statement about cultures is most appropriate?\nA. Cultures can be different and equally worthy of respect\nB. One culture must dominate\nC. Differences should be erased\nD. Only one language should be used\nAnswer: A\n59. Which activity can build global understanding among students?\nA. Cultural exchange projects\nB. Mocking accents\nC. Excluding newcomers\nD. Spreading stereotypes\nAnswer: A\n60. What does solidarity mean?\nA. Standing together to support others\nB. Ignoring others\nC. Competing against everyone\nD. Rejecting cooperation\nAnswer: A\n61. Which action is an example of kindness?\nA. Helping someone in need\nB. Laughing at someone in trouble\nC. Excluding someone\nD. Spreading rumours\nAnswer: A\n62. Which global challenge can be addressed through shared responsibility?\nA. Environmental protection\nB. Personal handwriting\nC. Individual shoe choice\nD. Private hobbies\nAnswer: A\n63. Which value supports peaceful communities?\nA. Trust\nB. Hatred\nC. Fear\nD. Discrimination\nAnswer: A\n64. What is intercultural understanding?\nA. Understanding and respecting different cultures\nB. Rejecting all cultures\nC. Forcing one culture\nD. Avoiding people\nAnswer: A\n65. Which action best represents \u201cthe world is one family\u201d?\nA. Helping people regardless of nationality\nB. Helping only relatives\nC. Ignoring strangers\nD. Helping only powerful groups\nAnswer: A\n66. Which of these can strengthen community bonds?\nA. Cooperation\nB. Stereotyping\nC. Exclusion\nD. Bullying\nAnswer: A\n67. What is one way technology can support global unity?\nA. Connecting people to share knowledge\nB. Spreading hate\nC. Creating misinformation\nD. Preventing communication\nAnswer: A\n68. Which online behaviour reflects the principle?\nA. Respectful communication\nB. Cyberbullying\nC. Hate speech\nD. Harassment\nAnswer: A\n69. Why should future generations matter?\nA. They will share the planet we care for today\nB. They have no connection to us\nC. Resources are unlimited\nD. The environment never changes\nAnswer: A\n70. Which is a peaceful response to conflict?\nA. Negotiation\nB. Violence\nC. Revenge\nD. Threats\nAnswer: A\n71. What does compassion involve?\nA. Concern for others' wellbeing\nB. Indifference\nC. Hostility\nD. Exclusion\nAnswer: A\n72. Which statement best connects Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam with environmentalism?\nA. Earth is a shared home and should be protected\nB. Nature belongs only to one nation\nC. Pollution affects nobody\nD. Resources should always be wasted\nAnswer: A\n73. What can sports teach about unity?\nA. Teamwork and respect\nB. Hatred\nC. Exclusion\nD. Prejudice\nAnswer: A\n74. Which approach is best for a diverse society?\nA. Inclusion and mutual respect\nB. Forced uniformity\nC. Discrimination\nD. Segregation\nAnswer: A\n75. What is the central human value behind universal family?\nA. Shared humanity\nB. Superiority\nC. Isolation\nD. Rivalry\nAnswer: A\n\nSection C \u2014 One-Word / Very Short Answer (50)\n1. The Sanskrit word meaning \u201cEarth/world\u201d in the phrase is _____.\nAnswer: Vasudha\n2. The Sanskrit word meaning \u201cfamily\u201d is _____.\nAnswer: Kutumbakam\n3. The language of the phrase Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam is _____.\nAnswer: Sanskrit\n4. The phrase is commonly translated as \u201cThe world is one _____.\u201d\nAnswer: family\n5. The text associated with the phrase is the Maha _____.\nAnswer: Upanishad\n6. A feeling of understanding another person's emotions is called _____.\nAnswer: Empathy\n7. Respect for different cultures is called cultural _____.\nAnswer: tolerance\n8. Working together for a common purpose is called _____.\nAnswer: cooperation\n9. Concern for others' wellbeing is called _____.\nAnswer: compassion\n10. Peaceful settlement of disagreements through discussion is called _____.\nAnswer: dialogue\n11. The quality of treating people fairly is _____.\nAnswer: equality\n12. Acceptance of different viewpoints is called _____.\nAnswer: tolerance\n13. A person aware of responsibilities toward the wider world is a global _____.\nAnswer: citizen\n14. The condition of living together peacefully is peaceful _____.\nAnswer: coexistence\n15. People helping each other during hardship show _____.\nAnswer: solidarity\n16. Different cultures existing together create cultural _____.\nAnswer: diversity\n17. The opposite of prejudice is often associated with _____.\nAnswer: equality\n18. Care for the natural world is environmental _____.\nAnswer: responsibility\n19. The common home of humanity is the _____.\nAnswer: Earth\n20. A peaceful method of resolving conflict is _____.\nAnswer: negotiation\n21. Respectful understanding between cultures is intercultural _____.\nAnswer: understanding\n22. Helping someone in need is an act of _____.\nAnswer: kindness\n23. The idea that all humans are connected is human _____.\nAnswer: unity\n24. The opposite of inclusion is _____.\nAnswer: exclusion\n25. Unfair judgment based on group identity is _____.\nAnswer: prejudice\n26. A harmful fixed belief about a group is a _____.\nAnswer: stereotype\n27. The process of accepting everyone into a group is _____.\nAnswer: inclusion\n28. Protecting natural resources is called _____.\nAnswer: conservation\n29. The peaceful absence of conflict is called _____.\nAnswer: peace\n30. The ability to understand another perspective is _____.\nAnswer: empathy\n31. A relationship based on mutual respect is _____.\nAnswer: harmony\n32. The sharing of knowledge between cultures is cultural _____.\nAnswer: exchange\n33. A shared feeling of belonging is _____.\nAnswer: community\n34. The principle of universal human family promotes _____.\nAnswer: brotherhood\n35. The practice of treating all people with dignity reflects human _____.\nAnswer: rights\n36. A common effort by many people is _____.\nAnswer: collaboration\n37. Careful use of resources is _____.\nAnswer: sustainability\n38. A person who belongs to a nation is a _____.\nAnswer: citizen\n39. The act of solving disagreements peacefully is conflict _____.\nAnswer: resolution\n40. Respectful acceptance of differences is _____.\nAnswer: tolerance\n41. A world connected through communication is a global _____.\nAnswer: community\n42. The feeling of concern for someone suffering is _____.\nAnswer: compassion\n43. A peaceful relationship among nations is international _____.\nAnswer: cooperation\n44. The idea that everyone deserves equal dignity reflects _____.\nAnswer: equality\n45. Working with others rather than against them is _____.\nAnswer: cooperation\n46. The reduction of hatred and prejudice supports social _____.\nAnswer: harmony\n47. Protecting the planet for future generations supports _____.\nAnswer: sustainability\n48. Helping people without discrimination reflects _____.\nAnswer: humanity\n49. The core message of Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam is universal _____.\nAnswer: brotherhood\nSuggested NYKRIS Use\n\u2022 Platform Skip: randomly select 1 question from the 200-question bank.\n\u2022 Level Skip: randomly select 3 questions": null, " require all 3 to be correct.\n\u2022 Avoid immediate repeats by tracking recently used question IDs.\n\u2022 Questions can be tagged by type and topic so the game can balance the random selection.": null}